"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/chinchaisuyus"}, {"title": "www.merriam-webster.com", "url": "https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/chinchasuyus"}]}, "The search didn't turn up anything on \"chayaco\" as a variant of \"ch'allay,\" so I can't confirm that link. The closest match is a related Spanish-American interjection.\n\n- **Achalay:** The Real Academia Española's historical dictionary lists achalay as an interjection of admiration, used affectionately or ironically, and traces it to the Quechua *achallay*, meaning roughly \"how nice, how good.\" It also lists achalau as a variant. Interjección, afectuosa o irónica, que expresa admiración, ponderación y deseo. (Del quechua achallay, per the RAE entry.)\n- **Chayay:** A Wiktionary entry gives the conjugation of a Quechua verb *chayay* (\"to arrive\"). It is unrelated to the interjection, and I found no link to *chayaco*.\n- **Chaya/chayaco:** Nothing on *chayaco* itself. A Wiktionary entry for *chaya* covers a Mexican shrub and a Quechua noun declension, with no reference to the interjection.\n\nIf you're looking for the Quechua source of *chayaco*, a Quechua dictionary or a regional Spanish dialect dictionary would be the better place to check."], "durationSeconds": 5.8785757919999995, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_016wTceQNy138411KqjPWWcV", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
