rg/damer/chaya"}, {"title": "CHALLA - Diccionario etimológico - DeChile", "url": "https://etimologias.dechile.net/?challa="}, {"title": "Carnavalito", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnavalito"}, {"title": "Ch’allay: la palabra de origen quechua que está en la esencia de los carnavales", "url": "https://sintesisdelaciudad.wordpress.com/2018/02/09/challay-la-palabra-de-origen-quechua-que-esta-en-la-esencia-de-los-carnavales/"}, {"title": "| ORIGEN E HISTORIA DE LA CHAYA |", "url": "https://www.lariojaantigua.com.ar/2018/02/origen-e-historia-de-la-chaya.html"}, {"title": "Chaya: una fiesta ancestral de La Rioja que contagia al país", "url": "https://meridianodigital.com.ar/chaya-una-fiesta-ancestral-de-la-rioja-que-contagia-al-pais/"}]}, "No Wikipedia article on the Chaya carnival turned up in these results. The sources that came back are dictionaries, regional articles, and an English Wikipedia page on a related Andean ritual.\n\n- **Etymology:** Several sources link \"chaya\" to the Quechua \"ch'allay,\" which means to sprinkle with water. One Spanish-language dictionary entry says the word comes from Quechua cháya, llegada, a different origin. The same dictionary family lists the Quechua root for \"challa\" as a separate homophone. A Chilean etymology site describes \"challa\" and \"chaya\" as two homophones, one meaning confetti-like paper bits thrown at carnival and the other a clay pot. Another source points out that the fact that south of the Maule it is pronounced chaya rather than challa is not proof against the etymology.\n- **Carnival game and ritual:** The Diccionario de americanismos describes a game played at carnival in Chile and northwestern Argentina, in which friends throw water, flour or streamers at each other. The same entry says the word can also name a ritual of pouring liquor on the ground as an offering to Pachamama.\n- **La Rioja, Argentina:** Local sources treat the Chaya as a carnival festival. One says the word comes from the Quechua voice meaning \"to sprinkle\" or \"to wet.\" Another notes that Pujllay is the god who presides over the Chaya ceremony. The festival is tied to Diaguita heritage and is held each year from the third Saturday of February.\n- **Ch'allay meaning:** An Argentine Quechua teacher, Carmelo Sardinas, is quoted as saying that \"ch'allay\" means \"asperjar\" (to sprinkle), and that chaya comes from it. He adds that La Rioja is the only northwestern province where the word has survived.\n\nThe one Wikipedia result, on the Llama Ch'uyay, is a different livestock-blessing ritual in Peru and is not about the carnival Chaya."], "durationSeconds": 6.987888542, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_0137kYhgRPwSgfoscXoiG3er", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
